# JavaScript Rules

## File Structure
Each JavaScript file should follow this structure:
1. File documentation header
2. Imports (grouped and ordered)
3. Constants and configurations
4. Helper functions
5. Main functionality
6. Exports

## File Header Template
```javascript
/**
 * @fileoverview ${fileDescription}
 * 
 * This file is part of the MONO design system tooling.
 * 
 * @author ${author}
 * @since ${date}
 * @module ${moduleName}
 */

'use strict';
```

## Function Documentation
```javascript
/**
 * ${functionDescription}
 * 
 * @param {${paramType}} ${paramName} - ${paramDescription}
 * @returns {${returnType}} ${returnDescription}
 * @throws {${errorType}} ${errorDescription}
 */
function ${functionName}(${paramName}) {
  ${body}
}
```

## Class Documentation
```javascript
/**
 * ${classDescription}
 * 
 * @class
 * @implements {${implements}}
 * @property {${propertyType}} ${propertyName} - ${propertyDescription}
 */
class ${className} {
  ${body}
}
```

## Import Order
1. Node.js built-in modules
2. External dependencies
3. Internal modules (absolute paths)
4. Local modules (relative paths)
5. Type imports

## Naming Conventions
- Files: kebab-case.js
- Classes: PascalCase
- Functions: camelCase
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Private properties: _camelCase
- Boolean variables: isPascalCase

## Code Style
- Indent: 2 spaces
- Quotes: single quotes for strings
- Semicolons: required
- Max line length: 80 characters
- Trailing commas: required in multiline
- Arrow function parentheses: always
- No unused variables or imports
- Use template literals for string interpolation

## Best Practices
- Use ES6+ features appropriately
- Prefer const over let
- Avoid var
- Use early returns
- Handle errors explicitly
- Use meaningful variable names
- Keep functions small and focused
- Document all public APIs
- Use TypeScript types when available
- Write pure functions when possible

## Error Handling
- Use descriptive error messages
- Document thrown errors
- Use custom error classes for specific errors
- Handle async errors with try/catch
- Provide error recovery strategies

## Testing
- Write unit tests for utilities
- Test error conditions
- Use meaningful test descriptions
- Group related tests
- Mock external dependencies

## CLI Commands
- Use descriptive command names
- Provide help documentation
- Validate input parameters
- Give meaningful feedback
- Handle edge cases gracefully

## Token Transformation
- Document transformation logic
- Validate token structure
- Provide clear error messages
- Handle missing or invalid tokens
- Document output format
